      {title:'Labels are necessary but not sufficient',body:'A correct label becomes useful traceability only when the record connects it to the right source material and transition. Removing the old identifier before the new relationship is verified or relying on cultivar appearance can permanently break harvest genealogy.',references:['REF-GS1-GLOBAL-TRACEABILITY']},
      {title:'Reconcile physical and recorded quantity',body:'Compare outgoing and receiving counts or weights using the supplied method and units. If values differ, preserve both original results and investigate through the controlled discrepancy process rather than silently changing one record, averaging values or selecting the number that makes the handoff balance.',references:['REF-MHRA-GXP-DATA-INTEGRITY']},
      {title:'Waste or hold does not disappear from the chain',body:'Material diverted to a hold, waste or other controlled status remains part of the material history as required by the local workflow. The stable principle is that removed or diverted quantity must not simply vanish from the source-to-output relationship.',references:['REF-GS1-GLOBAL-TRACEABILITY','REF-MHRA-GXP-DATA-INTEGRITY']}
